{"term":"cudzołożyć","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish verb meaning to commit adultery; to engage in sexual intercourse with someone outside marriage while one or both parties are married.","english_term":"to commit adultery","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Specifically denotes marital infidelity (adultery), narrower than registry concept 'fornication' (all extramarital sex); mapped there as the closest moral-religious condemnation frame. Standard in Catholic theology and historical Polish law. The noun form 'cudzołóstwo' (adultery) remains current in legal Polish."}
{"term":"cudzołożnik","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"adulterer","concept":"fornicator","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish masculine noun for an adulterer; a man who commits adultery or has sex with a married woman.","english_term":"adulterer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Masculine grammatical form; registry concept 'fornicator' covers all extramarital sex while this specifically denotes adultery. Common in historical legal documents and Catholic moral literature. Less frequently encountered than the verb 'cudzołożyć' in modern texts."}
{"term":"cudzołożnica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"adulteress","concept":"fornicator","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish feminine noun for an adulteress; a married woman who commits adultery or a woman who has sex with a married man.","english_term":"adulteress","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine grammatical form; historically carries heavier social and religious stigma than the masculine 'cudzołożnik' due to asymmetric Catholic shame norms around female sexual transgression. Less commonly encountered in texts than the masculine form."}
{"term":"lubieżność","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lasciviousness","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":25,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish literary noun for lasciviousness or lechery; the quality of being driven by excessive, shameless sexual desire openly expressed.","english_term":"lasciviousness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from 'lubieżny' (lascivious); still used in literary criticism, moral discourse, and journalism; elevated register alternative to cruder descriptors. Appears frequently in Polish translations of classical texts alongside 'rozwiązłość'."}
{"term":"cielesne żądze","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnal_desire","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish literary phrase for carnal desires; bodily lusts conceived as opposing spiritual aims, central to Catholic moral and ascetic discourse.","english_term":"carnal desires","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Idiomatic in Catholic theological Polish; 'cielesne' (bodily/carnal) signals the spiritual-vs-flesh dichotomy. Very well known in Catholic-majority Poland through religious education and homiletics. The adjective precedes the noun in the more common literary order."}
{"term":"chuć","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":32,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":38,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish noun for raw carnal lust or sexual appetite; one of the oldest native Slavic words for sexual desire in the language.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Rare in contemporary speech except in deliberately archaic or literary contexts; has a rawer, more visceral feel than the more common 'pożądanie' (yearning/desire). Known mainly to literature students and educated speakers; appears in Old Polish texts and early biblical translations."}
{"term":"żądze cielesne","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnal_desire","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish literary phrase for carnal desires; identical in meaning to 'cielesne żądze' with inverted word order producing a slightly more emphatic or poetic register.","english_term":"carnal desires","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Inverted syntax of 'cielesne żądze'; postpositive adjective 'cielesne' lends a more emphatic, literary tone. Both forms appear interchangeably in Catholic theological and literary Polish; the inversion is stylistic, not semantic."}
{"term":"rozpusta","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"debauchery","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":26,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish noun for debauchery, moral dissipation, or sexual licentiousness; excessive indulgence in sensual pleasures framed as moral degradation.","english_term":"debauchery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Still actively used in contemporary elevated Polish (journalism, literary criticism, sermons); derived from 'rozprząc' (to unharness/loosen); connotes moral dissolution through sensual excess rather than sexual activity alone. One of the more current words in this archaic set."}
{"term":"rozwiązłość","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"licentiousness","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":24,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":76,"recognition_pct_peak":76,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish noun for licentiousness or sexual promiscuity; the quality of being sexually and morally uninhibited beyond accepted social norms.","english_term":"licentiousness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'unloosedness/unboundedness'; derived from 'rozwiązać' (to untie/loosen). Used in religious-moral discourse and literary contexts; covers general moral licentiousness as well as specifically sexual promiscuity. Still current in elevated register Polish."}
{"term":"niecnota","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"libertine","concept":"libertine","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":66,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish noun for a person of low moral character; a scoundrel or villain, with implied sexual licentiousness in period usage.","english_term":"scoundrel, libertine","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'non-virtue/non-honor'; sexual connotation is secondary and context-dependent. In modern Polish used humorously or affectionately for a mischievous person, largely desexualized. Mapped to 'libertine' for the archaic sexual-moral sense in which it condemned morally loose individuals."}
{"term":"zmysłowe rozkosze","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sensual_pleasure","concept":"sensuality","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish literary phrase for sensual pleasures; the delights of the physical senses, especially sexual ones, framed in elevated aesthetic or philosophical discourse.","english_term":"sensual pleasures","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Entirely neutral in elevated literary register; 'zmysłowe' (from 'zmysły' = senses) covers all sensory pleasures but typically implies sexual pleasure in context. Common in Polish Baroque and Romantic literature and in philosophical treatments of hedonism."}
{"term":"cielesna rozkosz","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"bodily_pleasure","concept":"sensuality","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":12,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish literary phrase for carnal or bodily pleasure; the pleasure derived from physical or sexual experience, explicitly contrasted with spiritual fulfillment.","english_term":"carnal pleasure","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The adjective 'cielesna' (bodily/carnal) more explicitly signals the spiritual-vs-flesh theological framework than 'zmysłowe' (sensory); appears in theological and literary Polish to mark bodily pleasure as morally distinct from or inferior to spiritual pleasure."}
{"term":"sprośność","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"obscenity","concept":"obscenity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish noun for obscenity, lewdness, or smuttiness; sexually offensive or indecent content or behavior that violates public decency standards.","english_term":"obscenity, lewdness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Still actively used in contemporary Polish legal, journalistic, and moral discourse ('sprośne treści' = obscene content); colloquially also used for dirty jokes ('sprośny żart'). Derived from 'sprosić' (to make coarse/vile). More current than most words in this archaic set."}
{"term":"grzech cielesny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"carnal_sin","concept":"concupiscence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":86,"recognition_pct_peak":86,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish phrase for 'sin of the flesh'; any sexual transgression conceived as a sin against divine law, including fornication, adultery, lust, and masturbation.","english_term":"sin of the flesh","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Foundational Catholic concept in Polish religious discourse; covers the full range of sexual sin rather than just concupiscence (the tendency toward sin) per se — mapped there as the closest theological registry concept. Extremely well known in Catholic-majority Poland through catechism, confession, and homiletics."}
{"term":"łono","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"womb","concept":"uterus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":86,"recognition_pct_peak":86,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish noun meaning womb, lap, or bosom; in archaic and literary sexual contexts also used broadly for the female genital area.","english_term":"womb; (archaic) female genitalia","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Still used in modern Polish for womb ('łono matki' = mother's womb) and in liturgical expressions ('łono Abrahama' = Abraham's bosom). Archaic sexual sense covers the vulva/vagina/pubic area broadly without being crude in modern usage. Mapped to 'uterus' as primary anatomical referent; vulval/vaginal meanings require archaic literary context."}
{"term":"lędźwie","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"loins","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish plural noun for 'loins'; the pelvic or lumbar region associated with procreation and sexual vitality in biblical and literary usage.","english_term":"loins","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Appears in Polish biblical translations ('przepasać lędźwie' = 'gird your loins'); from Latin 'lumbi'. Refers to the lumbar/hip region and by extension the reproductive organs. Known mainly through religious education and literary study; very rarely used outside biblical or archaic literary contexts today."}
{"term":"dziewictwo","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"virginity","concept":"defloration","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":12,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish noun for virginity; the state of not having had sexual intercourse, especially in a woman, carrying strong cultural and religious value.","english_term":"virginity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"No registry concept exists for the state of virginity; mapped to 'defloration' as the most directly related concept (the act that ends this state). Carries strong Catholic cultural weight; 'stracić dziewictwo' = to lose one's virginity. Still current and well known in modern Polish."}
{"term":"cnota","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"chastity","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish noun meaning virtue or chastity; in archaic and literary Polish specifically denotes sexual purity and female chastity as a positive moral virtue.","english_term":"chastity, virtue","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary modern meaning is 'virtue' broadly; historically and in archaic usage specifically denoted female sexual virtue/purity. The phrase 'stracić cnotę' (to lose one's virtue) = to lose one's virginity. Mapped to 'sexual_abstinence' as closest registry concept; distinction is that 'cnota' names the virtuous state, not the practice of abstaining."}
{"term":"narząd sromotny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"genitalia_euphemism","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish phrase literally meaning 'shameful organ'; a historical euphemism for the genitals, especially female genitalia, expressing shame-based avoidance of direct anatomical naming.","english_term":"shameful organ (archaic euphemism for genitalia)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Found in old medical, moral, and religious Polish texts where direct naming of genitalia was taboo; 'sromotny' = shameful/disgraceful (from 'sromota' = shame). The root 'srom-' also yields the archaic anatomical term 'srom' (vulva), occasionally still used in formal medical Polish. Almost exclusively encountered by scholars today."}
{"term":"łódź Wenery","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vulva_euphemism","concept":"vulva","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":16,"recognition_pct_peak":28,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Rare Polish literary euphemism for the vulva; literally 'the boat of Venus', using classical mythology to aestheticize the external female genitalia.","english_term":"boat of Venus (poetic euphemism for vulva)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Extremely rare; found only in archaic erotic poetry and classical literary translations. 'Wenera' (Venus) was also the Polish root for venereal disease ('choroba weneryczna'), giving the word a dual literary resonance. The boat metaphor evokes the shape of the vulva. Almost exclusively encountered in scholarly or antiquarian contexts."}
{"term":"bezwstydnica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"shameless_woman","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":42,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":64,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish feminine noun for a shameless or immodest woman; applied to a woman who violates sexual modesty norms without remorse or embarrassment.","english_term":"shameless woman, brazen hussy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'without-shame-woman'; constructed from 'bez' (without) + 'wstyd' (shame) + feminine suffix. Operates within the honor-shame economy of traditional Polish Catholic culture. Still used, though increasingly colloquially or humorously rather than as serious moral condemnation. Masculine counterpart 'bezwstydnik' is less common."}
{"term":"lubieżnik","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"lecher","concept":"lecher","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":66,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish masculine noun for a lecher or lascivious man; a man consumed by excessive sexual desire who pursues gratification without moral restraint.","english_term":"lecher","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Masculine counterpart to 'lubieżnica'; derived from 'lubieżny' (lascivious). More literary than 'zboczeniec' (pervert) and less crude than contemporary insults; implies someone driven by lust rather than necessarily a predator. Still recognized in educated Polish speech."}
{"term":"siłownik","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis_euphemism","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":7,"recognition_pct_peak":16,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Polish euphemism for the penis, literally 'force-organ' or 'power-device'; exploiting the etymology of 'siła' (force/strength) to name the organ obliquely.","english_term":"(archaic) force-organ, penis euphemism","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Modern standard meaning of 'siłownik' is exclusively hydraulic or pneumatic actuator (mechanical engineering term). Use as a penis euphemism is extremely rare and archaic, appearing in old erotic texts. Recognition estimate applies to sexual meaning only — modern speakers knowing only the mechanical meaning would not recognize the sexual usage. Included for completeness of archaic vocabulary documentation."}
{"term":"miłosne igry","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"erotic_play","concept":"intimate_physical_play","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish literary phrase for amorous or erotic play; flirtatious or sexually intimate interaction conceived as a playful, game-like exchange.","english_term":"amorous play, love games","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Igry' is archaic plural of 'igra' (game/sport/play); the archaism marks this as distinctly literary rather than colloquial. Common in Polish Baroque and Romantic erotic poetry. Implies play-like erotic intimacy rather than full intercourse. Modern equivalent in colloquial register would be 'miłosne zabawy'."}
{"term":"cielesność","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnality","concept":"sensuality","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polish abstract noun for carnality or corporeality; the quality of being of the flesh or body, with sexuality understood as a dimension of physical human existence.","english_term":"carnality, corporeality","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Formed from 'cielesny' (bodily/carnal) + abstract suffix '-ość'. Used in philosophical, theological, and literary discourse; contrasted with 'duchowość' (spirituality). In sexual contexts implies the embodied, physical dimension of sexuality as a whole rather than any specific act. Mapped to 'sensuality' as the closest registry concept."}
